A||N||5||Bizquik||87 2.5 engine ticking ||||1||
Z||000000||Bizquik||11-14-2001||10:54 AM||||Well, the problem is just as described: the engine has had a ticking in it since it was taken out of storage this spring and putting in oil additives hasnt fixed it. If anyone has had this same thing happen, please let me know what parts might need replacing. thx <P>Jon||||reg||1
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||11-14-2001||11:19 AM||||depends where in the motor the ticking is coming from Could be lifters could be rockers are out of adjustment or wore out either one of these problems are easy enough to fix ||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnF||11-14-2001||11:27 AM||||Can also be an exhaust noise.<P>Use a listening device fashioned from a 2 or 3 ft piece of rubber hose or a stick (dowel from a plumber's plunger works great).  Stick one end on the engine where it seems the ticking is from, stick the other end next to an ear and simply listen.  Move the device around.  Usually works great for finding sources.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bizquik||11-14-2001||07:08 PM||||hey thx so much guys, i'll try that device. my father says its most likely not an exhaust leak sound||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-14-2001||07:21 PM||||the exhaust manifold on that engine is prone to cracking. I've been thru three. Biggest cause of cracks starting is water dumping on them when hot. The deck lid drops everything right on it.<P>Beyond that, the hose should give up the offending part.<P>Visually inspect the entire lenght of the serpent belt. It may have a spot comming apart.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierobruiser||11-14-2001||07:22 PM||||I bought my 88 Duke with 90K. I got it cheap and it sounded much like earlier GM 4 cylinders I had owned. I like to think it sounded like a sewing machine. i replaced the lifters and it quieted down a little but a short while later stopped dead on me, I thought the timing belt had gone like every other GM 4 cyl at about 70-90K. I found out that the 2.5 has timing gears and once they were replaced it runs like a very quiet, well oiled sewing machine.<P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
